---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="TUI Guide">
  <h1>TUI Guide</h1>
  <p>
    The claustre workbench is a terminal UI built with
    <a href="https://ratatui.rs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ratatui</a>. It
    uses a sidebar, main work buffer, inspector, and embedded session tabs for
    live agent terminals.
  </p>

  <h2>Workbench Layout</h2>
  <p>
    The root tab is split into four functional regions:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Area</th>
        <th>Content</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Sidebar</td>
        <td>Navigation, repositories, and focus summary</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Main buffer</td>
        <td><code>My Tasks</code>, <code>Sprint Board</code>, <code>Threads</code>, <code>Reviews</code>, <code>Agents</code>, or <code>Settings</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Inspector</td>
        <td><code>Issue</code>, <code>Diff</code>, <code>Runtime</code>, <code>Tests</code>, <code>Plan</code>, and <code>Attachments</code> for detail-heavy views like <code>Threads</code> and <code>Reviews</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Status / hints</td>
        <td>Status line, leader prompts, filter entry, delete confirmation, and key hints</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The sidebar lists workbench destinations and registered repositories. The
    main buffer changes with the selected view. <code>Threads</code> and
    <code>Reviews</code> keep a persistent inspector for diff/runtime/test
    context, while browse-first views like <code>My Tasks</code> and
    <code>Sprint Board</code> open detail overlays with <code>v</code>. Use
    <code>[</code> and <code>]</code> to resize the focused side pane, or drag
    the pane borders with the mouse.
  </p>
  <p>
    When GitHub is connected and the selected repository has been synced,
    <code>My Tasks</code> uses the local GitHub cache to show items assigned to
    the authenticated user. Task rows render as compact cards and include
    Project v2 metadata like <code>Type</code>, <code>Domain</code>,
    <code>Release</code>, <code>Priority</code>, and <code>Size</code> when
    those fields are present in the GitHub cache. If Claustre has no GitHub
    user or no synced repos yet, it falls back to the older local task list.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>Sprint Board</code> is now backed by GitHub Projects v2. Use
    <code>p</code> to open the repository switcher, <code>g</code> to open the
    GitHub board picker, <code>m</code> to choose a sprint or iteration value,
    <code>v</code> or <code>Enter</code> to open a task-detail dialog for the
    selected card, and <code>R</code> when you explicitly want to refresh
    project items from GitHub. Opening the board or changing the selected repo
    or board reads from Claustre's local GitHub cache first. Claustre shows
    board titles first and keeps the GitHub project number as secondary
    metadata. Both the
    repository picker and the board picker are searchable dialogs, so you can
    type to filter long project lists before confirming with <code>Enter</code>.
    The Project v2 <code>Status</code> field is mapped into the board columns
    defined in your config, and pressing <code>t</code> toggles between
    `Assigned to me` and `All sprint` scopes for the current board.
  </p>
  <p>
    Claustre now shows an animated spinner in the title and status line for
    user-triggered background work such as session preparation, GitHub
    connection, and GitHub installation loading. This makes slow operations
    visible instead of leaving the workbench looking idle or stuck.
  </p>
  <p>
    Launching coding work is thread-first. From <code>My Tasks</code> or
    <code>Sprint Board</code>, press <code>l</code> to open a launch modal
    where you pick the provider, optional runtime/workflow defaults, a thread
    title, and extra context. <code>Threads</code> is now the continuation
    workspace: press <code>n</code> to create a new ad hoc thread for the
    selected repo, just start typing to reply in the native chat surface,
    use <code>l</code> or <code>Enter</code> to continue it, and press
    <code>o</code> only when you want the live terminal tab.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>Reviews</code> is now a PR-first queue instead of a task filter. Use
    <code>t</code> to switch between <code>Authored PRs</code> and
    <code>Needs Review</code>. In the authored queue, <code>l</code> continues
    the selected pull request in a thread/worktree. In the needs-review queue,
    <code>l</code> launches an AI review thread with a review-specific prompt.
    The inspector can show the cached PR body and a remote
    <code>gh pr diff</code> preview even before a local thread exists.
  </p>
  <p>
    In <code>Settings</code>, focus the main pane with <code>2</code>. Use
    <code>j/k</code> to move between sections, then use <code>Enter</code> or
    the section-specific shortcuts shown in the bottom hint line to edit or
    toggle values.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use <code>Space t</code> for <code>My Tasks</code>, <code>Space b</code>
    for <code>Sprint Board</code>, <code>Space h</code> for
    <code>Threads</code>, <code>Space r</code> for <code>Reviews</code>,
    <code>Space a</code> for <code>Agents</code>, and <code>Space s</code> for
    <code>Settings</code>. Use <code>Space g</code> to jump directly to
    <code>Settings -&gt; GitHub</code> and <code>Space p</code> to jump to
    <code>Settings -&gt; AI Providers</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Session Tabs</h2>
  <p>
    After you confirm the launch modal, Claustre creates the worktree and live
    provider session for the thread. The primary surface stays inside the
    workbench: thread rail on the left, native chat in the middle, inspector on
    the right. Press <code>o</code> on the selected thread to open a session
    tab with embedded terminal panes. If the old PTY session is gone, Claustre
    relaunches the thread from its stored context before opening the fresh live
    session. The default
    layout is a shell pane on the left alongside the provider pane on the
    right. You can:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Split panes horizontally or vertically to add more shells</li>
    <li>Navigate between panes with keyboard shortcuts</li>
    <li>Close extra shell panes when you no longer need them</li>
    <li>Switch between the workbench root tab and session tabs</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Tab navigation uses <code>Ctrl+K</code> (previous tab) and
    <code>Ctrl+J</code> (next tab). Press <code>Ctrl+D</code> to detach from a
    session tab and return to the workbench root tab.
  </p>

